# Access security for the bench IOC.
#
# Unconditional by design: no UAG (user access group), no HAG (host access
# group), no calc conditions. Access security that depends on who is asking or
# where they are asking from would make the outcome of a test depend on the
# username and hostname of whatever machine ran it, which is exactly the kind of
# result that passes on a laptop and fails in CI. Everything below applies to
# every client equally.
#
# Rule levels: fields carry access level ASL0 or ASL1; VAL is ASL0. A rule
# covers fields at or below its level, so RULE(1, ...) covers every field, while
# a level-0 rule would cover only the ASL0 (VAL-like) fields.

# The group every record belongs to unless it names another. Fully open, which
# is what makes the one exception below mean something: a denied write here is
# a property of that channel, not of the IOC.
ASG(DEFAULT) {
    RULE(1, READ)
    RULE(1, WRITE)
}

# Readable, never writable. There is no WRITE rule at all -- the absence is the
# mechanism, since an omitted rule denies. A caput to a record in this group
# gets a CA "Write access denied" error raised by the server itself, before any
# record processing happens, which is the refusal an upstream client must be
# able to distinguish from a value that was accepted and then failed to take
# effect. Reads must keep working; a channel that vanished would be a different
# failure entirely and would prove nothing about write refusal.
ASG(protected) {
    RULE(1, READ)
}
